    "gold_solution": "```python\nfrom gurobipy import *\n\ndef solve_problem():\n    m = Model(\"MultiObjective_Transportation\")\n    m.setParam('TimeLimit', 300)\n\n    # --- Data ---\n    I = 5  # sources\n    J = 5  # destinations\n    K = 3  # max suppliers per store\n    w_cost = 0.6\n    w_emission = 0.4\n\n    supplies = [81, 226, 87, 192, 102]\n    demands = [30, 58, 18, 37, 50]\n    cost_matrix = [[21.01, 7.91, 27.56, 12.73, 16.44], [9.44, 18.74, 11.88, 4.72, 27.52], [16.63, 18.63, 9.42, 26.69, 26.25], [5.68, 28.02, 29.45, 29.46, 20.74], [16.9, 16.65, 18.26, 7.34, 27.32]]\n    emission_matrix = [[13.59, 6.16, 11.89, 14.37, 10.42], [18.33, 10.35, 9.56, 15.32, 15.73], [9.35, 8.31, 6.34, 3.01, 12.0], [20.0, 1.0, 12.41, 12.05, 17.52], [5.21, 15.21, 8.27, 13.13, 7.37]]\n    fixed_costs = [[66, 210, 138, 244, 273], [85, 280, 272, 233, 54], [107, 191, 120, 88, 230], [121, 280, 291, 122, 283], [50, 205, 164, 209, 113]]\n    moq = [[20, 6, 20, 19, 17], [13, 6, 11, 8, 5], [8, 18, 9, 9, 13], [18, 6, 16, 14, 19], [12, 7, 5, 20, 10]]\n\n    # Big-M per route\n    total_demand = sum(demands)\n    big_M = [[min(supplies[i], total_demand) for j in range(J)] for i in range(I)]\n\n    # --- Variables ---\n    # Continuous: shipment quantities\n    x = {}\n    for i in range(I):\n        for j in range(J):\n            x[(i,j)] = m.addVar(lb=0, vtype=GRB.CONTINUOUS, name=f\"Ship_S{i}_D{j}\")\n\n    # Binary: route-open indicators\n    y = {}\n    for i in range(I):\n        for j in range(J):\n            y[(i,j)] = m.addVar(vtype=GRB.BINARY, name=f\"RouteOpen_S{i}_D{j}\")\n\n    # --- Objective ---\n    # Weighted variable cost + fixed setup costs\n    m.setObjective(\n        quicksum(\n            (w_cost * cost_matrix[i][j] + w_emission * emission_matrix[i][j]) * x[(i,j)]\n            for i in range(I) for j in range(J)\n        )\n        + quicksum(fixed_costs[i][j] * y[(i,j)] for i in range(I) for j in range(J)),\n        GRB.MINIMIZE\n    )\n\n    # --- Constraints ---\n    # C1: Supply\n    for i in range(I):\n        m.addConstr(\n            quicksum(x[(i,j)] for j in range(J)) <= supplies[i],\n            name=f\"Supply_S{i}\")\n\n    # C2: Demand\n    for j in range(J):\n        m.addConstr(\n            quicksum(x[(i,j)] for i in range(I)) >= demands[j],\n            name=f\"Demand_D{j}\")\n\n    # C3: Linking \u2014 can only ship if route is open\n    for i in range(I):\n        for j in range(J):\n            m.addConstr(\n                x[(i,j)] <= big_M[i][j] * y[(i,j)],\n                name=f\"Link_S{i}_D{j}\")\n\n    # C4: Minimum order quantity\n    for i in range(I):\n        for j in range(J):\n            m.addConstr(\n                x[(i,j)] >= moq[i][j] * y[(i,j)],\n                name=f\"MOQ_S{i}_D{j}\")\n\n    # C5: Max suppliers per store\n    for j in range(J):\n        m.addConstr(\n            quicksum(y[(i,j)] for i in range(I)) <= K,\n            name=f\"MaxSupplier_D{j}\")\n\n    m.optimize()\n    return m\n```",
    "LLM_description": "To: VP, Supply Chain  \nFrom: Sustainability & Logistics Director  \nDate: Today  \nSubject: Balancing shipping cost and carbon impact under activation fees, minimum orders, and supplier limits\n\nContext\nOur distribution network must balance two imperatives: keep delivered costs competitive and reduce our carbon footprint. Every warehouse-to-store route has both a per-unit dollar cost and a per-unit CO2 emission rate, and these two measures often pull in opposite directions. Some low-cost routes carry higher emissions, while cleaner options can raise spend. To protect margin and make measurable progress toward our sustainability commitments, we will take an integrated view of cost and emissions when planning shipments.\n\nOperational rules that shape the plan\n- Route activation costs: Starting shipments on any warehouse\u2013store lane requires a one-time setup expense covering contracting, compliance, and physical readiness. A lane is either active or not; once active in the period, the setup cost is incurred regardless of volume moved.\n- Minimum order quantities: If a lane is activated, we must move at least a specified quantity on that lane to justify full-truck handling and avoid inefficient partial loads. If we cannot meet the minimum, the lane should remain inactive.\n- Supplier limits per store: Each store\u2019s receiving capacity and vendor management framework limit the number of different warehouses that can deliver to it in the period. This cap preserves dock throughput, scheduling reliability, and invoice simplicity.\n\nSupply and demand\n- Warehouses: Each warehouse has a finite inventory allocation available for outbound shipment this period. Shipments from a warehouse cannot exceed its available inventory.\n- Stores: Every store has a minimum demand requirement that must be met. Shorting stores is not acceptable; over-supplying does not help if it triggers higher costs or emissions without need.\n\nManagement priority\nLeadership has set weights that reflect the relative importance of cost versus emissions for variable shipping decisions this period. These weights apply only to per-unit transportation costs and per-unit emissions. Route activation fees are treated as fixed period costs; they are included in full when a lane is used and are not scaled by the weights. This approach makes clear trade-offs between cheaper, higher-emission routes and cleaner, potentially more expensive options while fully accounting for the real cash cost of opening lanes.\n\nGoal\nDetermine which lanes to activate and how much to ship on each active lane so that:\n- Warehouse inventory limits are respected\n- Each store\u2019s minimum demand is met\n- Activated lanes move at least their minimum order quantities\n- Each store receives from no more than its allowed number of warehouses\n- The blended total we track is as low as possible, where the blend equals the cost weight applied to total variable freight spend plus the emission weight applied to total CO2 emissions, with all activation fees added in full\n\nWhat success looks like\n- Fewer active lanes than the full network, focused on combinations that meet demand and stay within supplier limits\n- Mix of routes that reflect the established cost\u2013emission balance: cleaner lanes used where they deliver meaningful emission reductions at acceptable cost, and lower-cost lanes used where emissions impact is modest or where supply/demand constraints require it\n- Transparent rationale for each activated lane that includes the activation fee, the minimum volume commitment, and the resulting impact on both spend and CO2\n\nNext steps\n- Use the attached data template to populate supplies, demands, variable cost and emission rates by lane, activation fees, minimum order thresholds, and store supplier caps\n- Run scenario analyses to test sensitivity to the current weights and identify thresholds where lane choices change\n- Present a recommended activation and shipment plan with expected spend, emissions, and risk notes on warehouse utilization and store receiving operations\n\nANNEX \u2014 Data Template\n- Number of source warehouses: 5\n- Number of destination stores: 5\n- Warehouse supplies (units): Warehouse capacities: [81, 226, 87, 192, 102]\n- Store demands (units): Store demand requirements: [30, 58, 18, 37, 50]\n- Per-unit shipping cost matrix ($/unit): Shipping Costs ($/unit, 5 sources x 5 destinations):\nSource\\Dest | D0 | D1 | D2 | D3 | D4\n------------------------------------\nS0         |  21.01 |   7.91 |  27.56 |  12.73 |  16.44\nS1         |   9.44 |  18.74 |  11.88 |   4.72 |  27.52\nS2         |  16.63 |  18.63 |   9.42 |  26.69 |  26.25\nS3         |   5.68 |  28.02 |  29.45 |  29.46 |  20.74\nS4         |  16.90 |  16.65 |  18.26 |   7.34 |  27.32\n- Per-unit emission matrix (kg CO2/unit): CO2 Emissions (kg CO2/unit, 5 sources x 5 destinations):\nSource\\Dest | D0 | D1 | D2 | D3 | D4\n------------------------------------\nS0         |  13.59 |   6.16 |  11.89 |  14.37 |  10.42\nS1         |  18.33 |  10.35 |   9.56 |  15.32 |  15.73\nS2         |   9.35 |   8.31 |   6.34 |   3.01 |  12.00\nS3         |  20.00 |   1.00 |  12.41 |  12.05 |  17.52\nS4         |   5.21 |  15.21 |   8.27 |  13.13 |   7.37\n- Cost importance weight (variable cost): 60%\n- Emission importance weight (variable emissions): 40%\n- Route activation setup costs ($): Route Setup Costs ($, 5 sources x 5 destinations):\nSource\\Dest | D0 | D1 | D2 | D3 | D4\n------------------------------------\nS0         |     66 |    210 |    138 |    244 |    273\nS1         |     85 |    280 |    272 |    233 |     54\nS2         |    107 |    191 |    120 |     88 |    230\nS3         |    121 |    280 |    291 |    122 |    283\nS4         |     50 |    205 |    164 |    209 |    113\n- Minimum order quantities per active route (units): Minimum Order Quantities (units, 5 sources x 5 destinations):\nSource\\Dest | D0 | D1 | D2 | D3 | D4\n------------------------------------\nS0         |     20 |      6 |     20 |     19 |     17\nS1         |     13 |      6 |     11 |      8 |      5\nS2         |      8 |     18 |      9 |      9 |     13\nS3         |     18 |      6 |     16 |     14 |     19\nS4         |     12 |      7 |      5 |     20 |     10\n- Maximum suppliers per store: 3",
